We can transform this problem into a combinatorial problem using the concept of "gaps" between the chosen chairs. If we denote the chosen chairs by $c_1, c_2, \ldots, c_8$ such that $c_1 < c_2 < \cdots < c_8$, then the condition that no two people sit next to each other can be translated into the condition that $c_{i+1} \ge c_i + 2$ for all $i$. This can be simplified by letting $d_i = c_i - (i-1)$ for $i = 1, 2, \ldots, 8$. Then the condition becomes $d_{i+1} \ge d_i + 1$ for all $i$, which means $d_1, d_2, \ldots, d_8$ is a strictly increasing sequence of integers. Furthermore, since $c_1 \ge 1$ and $c_8 \le 16$, we have $d_1 \ge 1$ and $d_8 \le 9$.
